  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 236 AM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nThe transition away from summer is appearing in the synoptic\npattern taking shape over the next week. Upper-level ridging\ngenerally around the southern/central parts of the country will\nstill keep temperatures near to above normal in the broader\nregion, but split flow is noted in the guidance into next week.\nSouthern jet stream around the periphery of the ridge will carry\nembedded shortwaves and add more flow and shear to the \natmosphere. Shortwaves may bring showers or storms around \nSunday/Monday, but the models disagree whether ridging or \ntroughing will be more established to allow much. Either way, the \nconditions will be drier without concern for humidity causing \ndangerous heat stress.\n\nBy the time we get to the middle of next week, the ensemble of\nguidance shows a break down of the ridge and a more full-latitude\ntrough swinging from the western CONUS toward the central CONUS.\nBeing a bit early in the transitional time of year, confidence on\nthe timing of this features is very low. Official guidance leans\ninto a faster and more progressive low with a cold front and\nstorms in our area by Wednesday. Latest ECMWF paints the other\nextreme of the guidance - a slower low cutting off around the\nDesert Southwest and continued warm advection across our forecast\narea and region (rain to the northwest still) through Wednesday.\nWe will definitely have to monitor this potential system, as it\nmarks a change from the stagnant summer pattern and a potentially\nmore potent cool-season pattern. /NF/",
